Z||000000||fiero87||07-31-2000||02:40 PM||||Hi,<P>I recently had my 14 alloy's powdercoated white to match my car.  Turned out really good for only $100 bucks.  Anyway i bought some tires from a friend who smashed his car they are 180 70's .  I figured i would put these on the front and possibly get some wider ones for the rear.  What sizes would look good and fit on the standard 14 in rim? Not going for the ricer look, just something a bit wider for better traction and hookup at the track.  Any ideas? Thanks.  BTW if anyone wants to see the rims i can post pics.  <P>------------------<BR>Tim<BR>87 Fiero SC <BR>90 Daytona VNT(1 of 596)<BR>94 Duster (Winter ride)||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||07-31-2000||07:24 PM||||uuum I hope you not going to put 70 series tires on a Fiero. especially with a 180mm section width. the 14s i had on my SE were clad with Yokohama A509 which are no longer in production but the next best thing at close to the same price is the Yokohama AVS intermediate. either a 205-60-14 or 215-60-14 with the right suspension you'll be able to pull a full G. with those and a smooth hand on the wheel. GT Bastard now has them and I think another Forum member is using the 509s. If your lookin for a better than stock sporty ride (somthing you can drive to work and toss into a corner when you want too) check out <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tirerack.com</A>    If your REALLY into corner carving get a set of Yokohama A032s 205-60-14. SUPER sticky DOT approved slalom tires. LOVE THEM!! Won't last but MAYBE 4000miles.. (mine went way less) Stay away from 70 series tires. Your driving what should be a sports car and 70s are for Town Cars... not Fieros. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierocrazy||07-31-2000||10:07 PM||||I have 215/60's all the way around and they seem to do a good job, alittle hard to turn at slow speeds though||||reg||1
